===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|usually|with {{m|en|together}}}} To [[assemble]] (something real or [[figurative]]).
#: {{ux|en|These clues allowed us to '''piece''' together the solution to the mystery.}}
#* {{RQ:Fuller Church History|passage=His adversaries {{...}} '''pieced''' themselves together in a joint opposition against him.}}
# To make, enlarge, or repair, by the addition of a piece or pieces; to patch; often with ''out''.
#: {{ux|en|to '''piece''' a garment}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida|3|1|passage=You have broke it, cousin: and , by my life , you shall make it whole again ; you shall '''piece it out''' with a piece of your performance}}
# {{lb|en|slang}} To produce a work of [[graffiti]] more complex than a [[tag]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:William Upski Wimsatt|title=Bomb the Suburbs|year=1994|location=Chicago|publisher=The Subway and Elevated Press Company|page=7|edition=revised second|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bombsuburbs00wims/page/7/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-9643855-0-4|passage=“It didn't rain, so I decided to come '''piece''' with you.{{...}}” We never finished that piece.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Gregory J. Snyder|title=Graffiti Lives: Beyond the Tag in New York's Urban Underground|page=40|passage=It is incorrect to say that toys tag and masters '''piece'''; toys just do bad tags, bad throw-ups, and bad pieces.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Scape Martinez|title=GRAFF: The Art & Technique of Graffiti|page=124|passage=It is often used to collect other writer's tags, and future plans for bombing and '''piecing'''.}}

